  "summary": "Iconic Thai Buddha image style characterized by walking posture and distinctive flame-like halo.",
  "description": "Features a standing Buddha in walking pose with one foot forward, hands in Abhaya and Varada mudras, wearing simple monastic robes, and crowned with a flame-like halo. Developed during Sukhothai Kingdom's golden age, representing the idealized beauty and spiritual perfection of Thai Buddhist art.",
  "culturalContext": "The Sukhothai Walking Buddha (14th c.) — Thai bronze's supreme invention — freed the Buddha from static frontality: hip-shot contrapposto stride, one heel lifted, right hand in abhaya gesture, flame finial (rasmi) on the head, oval-face serenity with archaic smile. Wat Traphang Thong's bronzes and Sri Satchanalai castings defined the \"Sukhothai style\" that Ayutthaya inherited; the walking figure remains Thailand's national sculptural icon, quoted in every modern Thai temple bronze.",
